{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":7316682,"ns":0,"title":"Fitch notation","extract":"Fitch notation, also known as Fitch diagrams (named after Frederic Fitch), is a method of presenting natural deduction proofs in propositional calculus and first-order logics using a structured, line-by-line format that explicitly shows assumptions, inferences, and their scope. It was invented by Frederic Brenton Fitch in the 1930s and later popularized through his textbook Symbolic Logic (1952).","description":"Line-by-line system for natural deduction proofs","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}